## 7. Challenges / Difficulties Encountered in the Project

| # | Challenge | Solution |
| :-: | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | **Sensor calibration** — DHT11 requires 1–2 seconds warm-up per reading; soil sensor ADC values fluctuate significantly | Adopted multi-sample averaging and built a mapping table from analog values to actual moisture percentages |
| 2 | **MQTT connection stability** — Campus Wi-Fi is unreliable, causing intermittent disconnections between Arduino and the MQTT broker | Implemented auto-reconnection logic on the Arduino side and deployed a local MQTT broker to reduce network dependency |
| 3 | **Cross-platform data integration** — Arduino (C++), xiaozhi (ESP32 firmware), and OpenClaw (cloud agent) use entirely different technology stacks | Standardized on JSON data format across all components and adopted modular development per subsystem |
| 4 | **Scheduled task timing** — Ensuring accurate 6-hour interval reports without drift or missed executions | Implemented cron-based scheduling with error handling and automatic retry mechanisms |
| 5 | **AI prompt engineering** — Combining raw sensor data with plant care domain knowledge into effective LLM prompts | Iteratively refined prompts with domain-specific plant care context, including optimal ranges for each environmental parameter |
| 6 | **Arduino serial port dependency** — Arduino would not send MQTT data unless connected to a PC serial monitor; `Serial.begin()` blocks execution when no USB serial is present | Added a timeout delay: if serial is not ready within a threshold period, skip initialization and proceed directly to MQTT publishing — allowing standalone operation |

